    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (tid, '176. In emergency cardiac ultrasound, which view is obtained first to assess global cardiac function?', '["Apical 4-chamber", "Parasternal long axis (PLAX)", "Subcostal 4-chamber", "Suprasternal notch"]', 1, 'The parasternal long axis (PLAX) view is typically obtained first as it provides assessment of LV size and function, RV size, pericardial effusion, aortic root dimensions, and mitral/aortic valve motion.'),
    (tid, '177. On bedside echo, you see a large anechoic (black) space surrounding the heart with RV diastolic collapse. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pleural effusion", "Pericardial effusion with tamponade physiology", "Cardiomyopathy", "Normal finding"]', 1, 'Anechoic fluid surrounding the heart is pericardial effusion. RV diastolic collapse (and RA systolic collapse) indicates the effusion is causing tamponade physiology requiring pericardiocentesis.'),
    (tid, '178. What does the "D-sign" on parasternal short axis echo view indicate?', '["Normal LV function", "RV pressure/volume overload causing septal flattening (LV appears D-shaped)", "Pericardial effusion", "Aortic dissection"]', 1, 'The D-sign occurs when RV pressure overload (massive PE, pulmonary HTN) pushes the interventricular septum leftward, flattening it and causing the LV to appear D-shaped in cross-section.'),
    (tid, '179. In the RUSH exam (Rapid Ultrasound for Shock and Hypotension), what three components are assessed?', '["Heart, lungs, liver", "Pump (cardiac), Tank (volume status/IVC), Pipes (aorta/DVT)", "Brain, spine, abdomen", "Valves, chambers, vessels"]', 1, 'RUSH exam: Pump = cardiac function, pericardial effusion, RV strain. Tank = IVC size/collapsibility, lung sliding, FAST for free fluid. Pipes = aorta for AAA/dissection, DVT assessment.'),
    (tid, '180. An IVC diameter of <2.1 cm with >50% inspiratory collapse suggests what about right atrial pressure?', '["Elevated RAP (15-20 mmHg)", "Normal or low RAP (0-5 mmHg) suggesting fluid responsiveness", "Fixed elevated RAP", "Cannot be determined"]', 1, 'IVC <2.1 cm with >50% collapse estimates RAP 0-5 mmHg, suggesting the patient may be volume responsive. IVC >2.1 cm with <50% collapse suggests elevated RAP (10-20 mmHg).'),
    (tid, '181. Which ECG lead most closely correlates with the left anterior descending (LAD) coronary artery territory?', '["II, III, aVF", "V1-V4 (anterior leads)", "I, aVL", "V5-V6"]', 1, 'The LAD supplies the anterior wall and septum. ST changes in V1-V4 indicate LAD territory. V1-V2 = septal, V3-V4 = anterior. Proximal LAD occlusion may also cause changes in aVL.'),
    (tid, '182. ST depression in V1-V3 with tall R waves in a patient with chest pain should raise concern for what?', '["Anterior STEMI", "Posterior STEMI (obtain posterior leads V7-V9)", "Right ventricular infarction", "Pericarditis"]', 1, 'Isolated ST depression in V1-V3 is the mirror image of posterior ST elevation. Posterior leads (V7-V9) should be obtained. If ST elevation ≥0.5mm is found, it confirms posterior STEMI requiring reperfusion.'),
    (tid, '183. De Winter T-waves (upsloping ST depression with tall symmetric T waves in precordial leads) are equivalent to what?', '["Normal variant", "Proximal LAD occlusion (STEMI equivalent)", "Hyperkalemia", "Benign early repolarization"]', 1, 'De Winter pattern (1-3mm upsloping ST depression at J-point with tall, peaked, symmetric T waves in V1-V6) represents acute proximal LAD occlusion and is a STEMI equivalent requiring emergent PCI.'),
    (tid, '184. What is the significance of ST elevation in lead aVR with diffuse ST depression in multiple leads?', '["Pericarditis", "Left main coronary artery occlusion or severe three-vessel disease", "Normal variant", "Right ventricular strain"]', 1, 'ST elevation in aVR with widespread ST depression suggests left main or proximal LAD occlusion, or severe three-vessel disease causing global subendocardial ischemia. This is a high-mortality emergency.'),
    (tid, '185. A patient presents with chest pain and ECG shows a QTc of 280 ms. What condition does a short QT interval suggest?', '["Long QT syndrome", "Short QT syndrome with risk of VF and sudden death", "Normal finding", "Hypothyroidism"]', 1, 'Short QT syndrome (QTc <340ms) is a rare channelopathy associated with AF, VT/VF, and sudden cardiac death. The short refractory period creates a substrate for re-entrant arrhythmias.'),
    (tid, '186. What is the significance of Osborn waves (J waves) on ECG?', '["Hyperkalemia", "Hypothermia (core temperature typically <32°C)", "Hypercalcemia", "Digitalis effect"]', 1, 'Osborn waves are positive deflections at the J-point, most prominent in leads II and V5-V6. They are classically associated with hypothermia but can also occur in hypercalcemia, brain injury, and Brugada.'),
    (tid, '187. Early repolarization pattern on ECG shows ST elevation with a "fish-hook" J-point. When is this concerning?', '["Always benign", "When associated with inferior and lateral leads in a patient with syncope or family history of sudden death", "Only in elderly patients", "Never concerning"]', 1, 'Once considered universally benign, early repolarization in inferior/lateral leads with J-point elevation >2mm and horizontal/descending ST segments has been associated with increased risk of VF (Haissaguerre 2008).'),
    (tid, '188. What does a "strain pattern" on ECG (ST depression with asymmetric T-wave inversion in lateral leads) typically indicate?', '["Acute MI", "LV hypertrophy with repolarization abnormality", "Pulmonary embolism", "Pericarditis"]', 1, 'LVH strain shows downsloping ST depression with asymmetric T-wave inversion in leads with tall R waves (V5-V6, I, aVL). It represents repolarization abnormality from hypertrophy, not acute ischemia.'),
    (tid, '189. S1Q3T3 pattern on ECG is classically associated with which cardiac emergency?', '["Inferior MI", "Acute right heart strain from pulmonary embolism", "Pericarditis", "Aortic dissection"]', 1, 'S1Q3T3 (deep S in lead I, Q wave and inverted T in lead III) suggests acute right heart strain from PE. Though not sensitive (~20%), it is fairly specific. Other PE findings: sinus tachycardia, RBBB, right axis deviation.'),
    (tid, '190. What does the term "tombstone ST elevation" describe on ECG?', '["Benign early repolarization", "Massive transmural ischemia with convex ST elevation merging with T wave, resembling a tombstone shape", "Normal variant", "Pericarditis pattern"]', 1, 'Tombstone morphology shows massive convex ST elevation where the ST segment merges with the T wave creating a monophasic shape. It indicates extensive transmural ischemia with high mortality and mandates emergent PCI.'),
    (tid, '191. In point-of-care cardiac ultrasound, what is the E-point septal separation (EPSS) and what does it estimate?', '["Valve area", "An M-mode measurement of mitral valve excursion estimating LV ejection fraction; EPSS >7mm suggests reduced EF", "RV function", "Pericardial fluid volume"]', 1, 'EPSS measures the minimum distance between the anterior mitral leaflet and the interventricular septum during diastole. EPSS >7mm correlates with EF <50% and >10mm with EF <30%. Quick bedside LV function estimate.'),
    (tid, '192. Which ultrasound finding in the subcostal view helps differentiate pericardial effusion from left pleural effusion?', '["Fluid anterior to the descending aorta = pericardial; posterior to the aorta = pleural", "Size of the effusion", "Patient position", "They cannot be differentiated"]', 0, 'In the parasternal long axis view, pericardial effusion tracks anterior to the descending aorta while pleural effusion tracks posterior to it. This is the most reliable distinguishing feature on bedside echo.'),
    (tid, '193. What is the focused cardiac ultrasound finding that most reliably predicts cardiac arrest survivability?', '["IVC diameter", "Organized cardiac activity (wall motion) versus cardiac standstill", "Valve motion", "Chamber size"]', 1, 'Cardiac standstill (no ventricular wall motion) on ultrasound during cardiac arrest has >95% negative predictive value for survival. Organized cardiac activity (pseudo-PEA) has a much better prognosis.'),
    (tid, '194. A patient with a pacemaker has inappropriate sensing of skeletal muscle activity causing pacing inhibition. What is this called?', '["Failure to capture", "Oversensing", "Undersensing", "Pacemaker syndrome"]', 1, 'Oversensing occurs when the pacemaker detects non-cardiac signals (skeletal myopotentials, EMI, T waves) and inappropriately inhibits pacing output. It can cause symptomatic bradycardia or pauses.'),
    (tid, '195. What is pacemaker-mediated tachycardia (PMT)?', '["VT caused by the pacemaker", "A re-entrant tachycardia where the pacemaker acts as the anterograde limb and a native pathway as the retrograde limb", "AF detected by the pacemaker", "Lead fracture"]', 1, 'PMT occurs in dual-chamber pacemakers when a retrograde P wave is sensed by the atrial lead, triggering ventricular pacing, which again conducts retrograde, creating an endless loop tachycardia. Magnet application terminates it.'),
    (tid, '196. Placing a magnet over a pacemaker causes what effect?', '["Turns off the pacemaker", "Switches to asynchronous (fixed-rate) pacing mode, disabling sensing", "Increases pacing rate to 120 bpm", "Resets the device to factory settings"]', 1, 'A magnet over a pacemaker activates asynchronous (AOO/VOO/DOO) mode at a fixed rate, disabling sensing. This is used to override oversensing or EMI inhibition. The rate varies by manufacturer.'),
    (tid, '197. Placing a magnet over an ICD causes what effect?', '["Turns off pacing", "Disables tachyarrhythmia detection and shock therapy (pacing function continues)", "Delivers a shock", "Resets the device"]', 1, 'A magnet over an ICD suspends tachyarrhythmia therapies (shocks and antitachycardia pacing) but does NOT affect bradycardia pacing. This is used when inappropriate shocks are occurring.'),
    (tid, '198. What is the most accurate bedside method to confirm endotracheal tube placement during cardiac arrest?', '["Auscultation of breath sounds", "Continuous waveform capnography (ETCO2)", "Chest X-ray", "Condensation in the ET tube"]', 1, 'Continuous waveform capnography is the gold standard for confirming and monitoring ETT placement during CPR. Absence of ETCO2 waveform suggests esophageal intubation or very low cardiac output.'),
    (tid, '199. In a trauma patient with Beck triad and PEA, bedside echo shows pericardial effusion. Pericardiocentesis yields non-clotting blood. If hemodynamics do not improve, what is the next step?', '["Repeat pericardiocentesis", "Emergency department thoracotomy (EDT)", "CT scan", "IV fluid bolus only"]', 1, 'Traumatic cardiac tamponade with hemodynamic instability unresponsive to pericardiocentesis requires EDT for direct cardiac repair (especially penetrating trauma). EDT allows direct visualization, clamping of bleeding, and open cardiac massage.'),
    (tid, '200. What are the indications for emergency department thoracotomy (EDT)?', '["All cardiac arrest patients", "Penetrating thoracic trauma with recent loss of vital signs (<15 min) or refractory tamponade", "Blunt trauma with prolonged arrest", "Stable penetrating chest wound"]', 1, 'EDT is indicated for penetrating thoracic injuries with witnessed arrest and <15 min of CPR, or penetrating cardiac tamponade unresponsive to pericardiocentesis. Blunt trauma EDT has very poor outcomes.');
